[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2608760000-jvm-free-multi-route-http-service-host"
  :adr/title "amu: a JVM-free, multi-route HTTP host for compiled Kotoba guests (kotoba/http-service)"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/body
  "## Context

The owner asked to remove the JVM dependency from `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app`
(a full JVM Clojure webapp) and run it via the `kotoba` CLI, authorizing new
libraries in `kotoba-lang` where needed.

Investigation (this session) found:

- `kotoba-lang/amu`'s `runtime/wasi-service.mjs` is a real, working JVM-free
  HTTP host: a Node `node:http` server that instantiates a compiled `.kotoba`
  WASM module via WASI (`runtime/wasi-host.mjs`) and dispatches to it. It is
  extremely narrow: exactly one route (`POST /v1/run`), a request schema
  locked to `{entry, args, format}` with `args` bounded to 5 canonical i64
  decimal strings. No arbitrary JSON body, no multiple routes, no sessions.
- `kotoba-lang/mesh` (`HTTP mesh-route runtime for compiled Kotoba guests`) is
  the closest prior art for the DESIGN PATTERN, not a solution to the JVM
  problem: its `route.kotoba` decision core (compiled ahead of time, executed
  on every request) is real and landed (ADR-2608120200), but its host
  (`node.clj`) runs on JVM + Chicory. Reusing mesh's route.kotoba source
  as-is was considered and rejected: it hard-codes murakumo's fixed
  `/mesh/http/<route>` prefix scheme, a different product than a general
  reusable multi-route table.
- `kotoba-lang/kotobase-server`'s own README documents that this workspace's
  flagship production server (kotobase.net) has NOT achieved a JVM/Rust-free
  replacement either (ADR-2607072000 no-Rust policy, pinned Rust binary,
  \"no cljc successor\" yet). The gap this ADR closes is real, and closing it
  here does not imply kotobase.net or cloud-itonami-app are now JVM-free.
- No existing repo already provides this (checked via
  `nbb scripts/repo-search.cljs http serve route multi-route` and
  `json codec document-json`; closest hits were `mesh` (JVM-hosted),
  `capability-http-fetch`/`capability-http-post`/`capability-component-http`
  (outbound-only atomic capability contracts, not a serving host), and
  `kotoba-lang/json` (a `.cljc` JSON codec with no `.kotoba` port yet).

Per this workspace's `.kotoba` runtime-priority rule (`kotoba wasm runtime >
clojurewasm > ClojureScript > nbb`, JVM/bb demoted to last resort) and the
'既定は restricted ESM' section of CLAUDE.md, a Node-hosted `:js-kotoba-v1`
restricted-ESM module (`amu compile --target js`) is an explicitly sanctioned,
already-proven way to run compiled Kotoba guests without a JVM at RUNTIME
(the compiler itself still needs a JDK to produce the `.mjs` artifact --
that is a build-time tool dependency, not a runtime one, and is explicitly
out of scope for ADR-2607198300's 'no JVM at runtime' rule, same as gcc
needing to run somewhere to build a native binary).

Empirical finding this session: `--target js` (`:js-kotoba-v1`) turns out to
be a BETTER fit for an HTTP host than the WASI/wasm32 path `wasi-service.mjs`
already uses. A restricted-ESM instance exposes plain JS values directly
(strings pass as plain JS strings, i64 as BigInt, keywords as `\":kw\"`
strings, bool as plain bool -- confirmed by compiling and calling a probe
module) and is fuel-metered per `instantiateKotoba()` call (confirmed via
`amu/runtime/dom-driver.mjs`'s own documented behaviour: a shared instance
dies of `fuel-exhausted` after ~8 calls). A guest call therefore either
returns or traps -- it cannot spin the event loop forever -- so no
`worker_threads.Worker` isolation is needed, unlike `wasi-service.mjs`.

## Decision

Add `runtime/http-service.mjs` to `kotoba-lang/amu`: a multi-route, JSON-body
HTTP host for `:js-kotoba-v1` guests, following the SAME decision/mechanism
split `kotoba-lang/mesh`'s `route.kotoba` established (ADR-2608120200), ported
onto this JVM-free host rather than reusing mesh's fixed-prefix core:

- `runtime/http/route-decide.kotoba`, compiled ahead of time to
  `runtime/http/route-decide.mjs` and called on every request, decides:
  which built-in kind a (method, path) pair is (`:health`, `:metrics`,
  `:route`), what outcome a request produces given a HOST-SUPPLIED BOOLEAN
  for 'is this route bound' and 'did the guest answer' (never the route
  table itself -- mirrors mesh's ADR-2608112100 measured boundary: a core
  handed one decision's worth of scalars delegates; a core handed a growing
  collection does not), and what HTTP status an outcome gets.
- `runtime/http-service.mjs` owns the route TABLE (grows with deployment),
  Node socket/JSON mechanics, sealed sha256 digest verification of every
  loaded guest module (refuses to import a module whose bytes don't hash to
  the pinned digest), a hard refusal of any module declaring a non-empty
  `requiredCapabilities` (this host grants zero capabilities to any guest --
  every route handler and the decision core itself are `kotoba/pure`), and
  bounded concurrency/body-size (65536 bytes, `stringLimits.valueBytes`'s own
  measured ceiling on this target).
- Body/response values cross the boundary as `:string` (not `:document`):
  the host validates the request body and the guest's returned string are
  each syntactically valid JSON (via `JSON.parse`, reject on failure) and
  passes/returns the exact original bytes -- no restructuring. This is a
  genuine generalization from `wasi-service.mjs`'s 5-bounded-i64-args scheme
  to arbitrary-size JSON bodies, proven end to end with a `POST /echo` route
  that round-trips an arbitrary JSON object byte-for-byte.

Landed via https://github.com/kotoba-lang/amu/pull/680 (merge commit
391124d81b447884850811da9d59141eacf05a33 on `kotoba-lang/amu` main). The
`amu` west pin did not need advancing here: by the time this ADR landed, a
separate routine had already moved the pin to a6ccf157111f, which
`gh api repos/kotoba-lang/amu/compare/391124d8...a6ccf157111f` confirms is
2 commits strictly ahead of (0 behind) this PR's merge commit -- i.e. this
change is already included in the pinned checkout. Verified with
`gh api repos/kotoba-lang/amu/compare/a6ccf157111f...main` -> `identical`,
so the pin is also not stale against amu's current tip.

## Verification (real, not simulated)

`scripts/http-service-e2e.cljs` (the repeatable, real test): recompiles
`route-decide.kotoba` fresh and fails loudly on any drift from the shipped
`route-decide.mjs`'s `sourceDigest`; compiles two disposable fixtures fresh
every run (a demo guest exporting `health`/`echo`, and a decision-core
DOUBLE with deliberately different status mapping); starts a real
`node:http` server; and fires real HTTP requests via `fetch` against it.
18 assertions, all passing, including:

- real end-to-end dispatch: `GET /health` -> 200 with a fixed JSON body,
  `POST /echo` -> 200 round-tripping an arbitrary JSON object byte-for-byte,
  `GET /nope` -> 404 with `not-found` (an unregistered route),
  `POST /health` (wrong method) -> 404, malformed JSON body -> 400,
  oversized body -> 413, `/healthz` and `/metrics` host built-ins unaffected
  by the route table.
- **delegation** (the test that actually distinguishes 'calls the compiled
  core' from 'has its own hardcoded cond', mirroring mesh's own
  `kotoba_oracle_test.clj` philosophy): swapping the decision core for the
  double changes an unbound route's status from 404 to 410 -- proving the
  host really asks the core, not a copy of its logic.
- a module with a digest mismatch is refused at STARTUP, not silently served.
- a module declaring a required capability (reused `examples/capability.kotoba`
  + `examples/capability-policy.edn`, which compiles to `requiredCapabilities:
  [7]` even on this target) is refused at startup -- this host really grants
  nothing, it is not merely undocumented that it could.

Separately, real `curl` requests were run against a standalone
`node runtime/http-service.mjs` process (not just the in-process test
harness) for `GET /health`, `POST /echo`, `GET /nope`, `GET /healthz` --
transcript recorded in the landing PR. `ps` was checked before/during/after
to confirm no `java` process is running while the server answers requests
(java only ran during the earlier compile step).

## Honest scope statement -- how far this is from 'cloud-itonami-app runs without a JVM'

**Not close. This lands zero application routes.** `cloud-itonami-app`'s
`server.clj` has a large route surface (auth, mail sync, drive sync, an LLM
tool-calling loop, wallet/payments, WebAuthn ceremonies, OAuth redirects) --
none of it was touched, ported, or even inventoried route-by-route this
session. What exists now is a bare mechanism that can serve `N` routes
whose handlers are pure `:string -> :string` Kotoba functions with no
sessions, no cookies, no streaming, and no in-guest structural JSON parsing
(the body crosses as a validated-syntactically-JSON string; a guest that
wants to inspect individual fields must do its own string work today, or
wait for `kotoba-lang/json`'s `.cljc` JSON codec to get a `.kotoba` port --
that port does not exist yet and is not started by this ADR).

Concretely unaddressed and each requiring separate, likely substantial,
future ADRs before any real progress on `cloud-itonami-app`:

- **WebAuthn ceremonies** -- not investigated at all this session. Almost
  certainly needs a `.kotoba`-callable crypto capability (COSE/CBOR
  attestation verification) this workspace does not yet have compiled to
  any Kotoba target.
- **OAuth redirects / session cookies** -- this host has zero cookie/session
  primitives; sessions would need a new capability-gated store, not a pure
  guest function.
- **Mail sync / drive sync** -- these are outbound integrations
  (`capability-http-fetch`/`capability-http-post` exist as atomic
  authorities, but no guest-callable client library compiled to `.kotoba`
  wires them up yet).
- **LLM tool-calling loop** -- no `.kotoba` LLM client exists; this is a
  meaningfully sized separate build.
- **Wallet/payments** -- explicitly out of scope for any agent per this
  workspace's safety floor (no autonomous fund movement).
- **Structural JSON parsing inside a guest** -- named above; today a guest
  can only pass through or emit fixed JSON text, not inspect arbitrary keys.

## Proposed next slice (scoped, not started)

A single real `cloud-itonami-app` read-only GET endpoint with NO auth, NO
session, and a fixed response shape (e.g. a health/status endpoint that
currently exists in `server.clj`) ported end-to-end onto this host, as the
first proof that a real application route -- not a synthetic demo -- can run
on `http-service.mjs`. That slice would also be the first real pressure test
of the 'body crosses as a validated-JSON string, not a structured value'
design choice, and should decide whether the `kotoba-lang/json` `.cljc` port
to `.kotoba` needs to happen before or after it.

## Consequences

- `kotoba-lang/amu` gains a second, general-purpose HTTP host alongside the
  narrow `wasi-service.mjs`; both remain (different guest compile targets,
  different tradeoffs -- wasi-service.mjs's Worker-thread isolation may still
  be preferable for guests that need a hard OS-level time cutoff rather than
  a fuel budget).
  - No new capability-authority repo was created (`capability-http-serve` /
    `capability-net-listen` were considered per the naming-rules check in
    `manifest/repository-rules.edn`'s `capability-` prefix declaration, which
    requires `:execution :none` -- a contract-only repo -- and this host is
    execution, not a contract). A future slice where GUESTS (not the host)
    need direct listen/accept authority would be the trigger to add one;
    today's guests are capability-free pure functions, so none was needed.

## Addendum (2026-08-29) -- the named next slice: one real route + admission-decision parity

Landed via https://github.com/kotoba-lang/amu/pull/683 (merge commit
8a5152fa8a93e129e9ae097cd9f0fe9822ae9edc on `kotoba-lang/amu` main; west pin
advanced to that commit the same session). `cloud-itonami-app` was NOT
modified -- its source was only read (byte-for-byte, unmodified) and its
already-shipped production code path was only run, never edited.

### What was built

- `examples/cloud-itonami-health-route.kotoba`: a new `kotoba/pure` response
  guest carrying the REAL `cloud.itonami.app.health.v1` JSON shape read from
  `cloud-itonami-app`'s `server.clj` (`process-liveness?` branch, confirmed
  by curling a live `cloud-itonami-app` process on `127.0.0.1:1338` the same
  session and cross-reading `server.clj` directly). `ok`, `service`, and
  `schema` are the real, fixed constants. `store` and `drive-store` on a real
  server are `(config/store-fingerprint)` and
  `(name (documents/selected-store-kind))` -- this process's own data
  directory fingerprint and selected drive backend, which a standalone demo
  host has neither of. Rather than fabricate a plausible-looking value for
  either, both fields literally say `\"representative-...\"` and the file's
  own header spells out exactly what is real vs. representative.
- `scripts/cloud-itonami-health-route-e2e.cljs` +
  `test/http/cloud_itonami_health_test.mjs`: wires `GET /health` onto a real
  `http-service.mjs` instance (reusing the existing, UNMODIFIED
  `runtime/http/route-decide.mjs` shared decide core -- the same one the
  original synthetic demo used) and fires real HTTP requests over a real
  socket. 6/6 assertions pass: 200 + correct shape on `GET /health`, 404 on
  `POST /health` (wrong method) and `GET /nope`, 200 on the host's own
  `/healthz` (unaffected by the route table).
- `scripts/cloud-itonami-health-parity.cljs`: the slice's actual point.
  Compiles `cloud-itonami-app`'s existing, UNMODIFIED
  `src/cloud/itonami/app/health_core.kotoba` (read from a checkout named by
  `--app-dir`/`CLOUD_ITONAMI_APP_DIR`) to this repo's `:js-kotoba-v1` target
  with the same `amu compile` used everywhere else in this ADR, then checks
  its `health-route?` export against the REAL production oracle path --
  `cloud.itonami.app.health/health-route?`, run UNMODIFIED via `clojure -M`
  inside that checkout, which itself calls `kotoba.kir/execute` against the
  shipped `resources/cloud/itonami/app/oracle/health.kir.edn` -- across 21
  `(method, path)` cases: the one positive (`GET /health`) plus 20 negatives
  (wrong method incl. case, wrong path incl. case/trailing-slash/whitespace/
  query-string/path-traversal-string, unrelated paths, all-empty). **21/21
  agree.** The mismatch-detection path itself was verified to actually fire,
  not just report a clean PASS by construction: one run with the JS side's
  `(method, path)` arguments deliberately swapped correctly reported 1/21
  MISMATCH on exactly the case that swap would break (`GET /health`), 20/21
  AGREE elsewhere.

### What this does, and does not, establish

The `GET /health` route now served by `http-service.mjs` is gated by THAT
host's own shared `route-decide.kotoba` (table lookup), not by
`health_core.kotoba`. The parity result is a narrower, more precise claim,
stated deliberately narrowly: IF a host wired `health_core.kotoba`'s own
`health-route?` as its admission gate the way `cloud-itonami-app`'s real
production server does today (via the JVM/KIR-interpreter oracle), an
`amu`-compiled `:js-kotoba-v1` version of the exact same source would answer
identically on every input tried. That is real evidence the admission
DECISION survives the runtime swap (JVM/KIR-interpreter -> Node/restricted-
ESM) for this one route -- it is not evidence that `cloud-itonami-app` is
closer to running without a JVM. The gap named in the base ADR's honest
scope statement is unchanged: no session, no auth, no cookies, no streaming,
no structural JSON parsing in-guest, and every other route in `server.clj`
(auth, mail/drive sync, the LLM tool-calling loop, WebAuthn, OAuth,
wallet/payments) is untouched. One route's admission-decision parity is not
a served, production-equivalent `/health` endpoint replacing the JVM one,
let alone progress on the rest of the application.\n## Addendum 2 (2026-08-29) -- the second route: RFC 9728 protected-resource metadata\n\nSame claim as Addendum 1, second route. `cloud-itonami-app`'s real,\nunmodified `oauth_resource_core.kotoba` -- the RFC 9728 protected-resource\ngate for `GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp`, which its\nproduction server calls through `cloud.itonami.app.kotoba-oracle` -- gives\nIDENTICAL admission answers under the JVM/KIR-interpreter oracle path it\nruns today and under this repo's `:js-kotoba-v1` target hosted JVM-free on\nNode.\n\nMeasured 2026-08-29: **28/28 cases agree**, positive case admitted by both.\nLanded as `kotoba-lang/amu` merge `e4a1ef20a77da481c32cf5e0ee2a9f5edd908bbf`.\n\nFiles: `scripts/cloud-itonami-oauth-resource-parity.cljs` (the parity\nclaim), `examples/cloud-itonami-oauth-resource-route.kotoba` +\n`test/http/cloud_itonami_oauth_resource_test.mjs` +\n`scripts/cloud-itonami-oauth-resource-route-e2e.cljs` (the served route,\n11/11). Purely additive: no existing file changed, and Addendum 1's e2e was\nre-run green.\n\n### What this battery could test that Addendum 1's could not\n\nCross-admission. These two cores are siblings by design --\n`oauth_resource_core.kotoba`'s own header calls itself \"Sibling of\n`health_core.kotoba` ... not a second liveness probe\" -- so the battery\nincludes `GET /health` and `GET /healthz` as inputs. A core that admitted\nits sibling's path would be a real defect that a battery of only its own\npath could never see. Both correctly answer false, under both runtimes.\n\n### Two floors this parity script adds over Addendum 1's\n\n- **The positive case must be admitted by BOTH runtimes**, checked\n  separately from mismatch counting. A battery on which both sides answer\n  `false` everywhere \"agrees\" with itself while proving nothing -- exactly\n  the shape CLAUDE.md's \"測れなかった検査が、測って問題が無かった検査と\n  同じ値を返す\" rule warns about. Addendum 1's script would have reported\n  PASS in that state; this one reports FAIL and says why.\n- **The detector is mutation-verified, not assumed.** A mutant that wrongly\n  admits exactly one negative (the trailing-slash case) was run and\n  reported `FAIL -- 1/28 mismatches` naming exactly that case, exit 1.\n\n### What is real vs. representative in the served body, and why the gap is\n### wider here than for /health\n\nThe shape comes from `cloud.itonami.app.oauth-resource/metadata*`, which\nbuilds five keys. Three are fixed constants in that file, copied verbatim\nand asserted exactly: `resource_name` (\"Itonami Cloud MCP\"),\n`bearer_methods_supported` (`[\"header\"]`), `scopes_supported` (the `scopes`\ndef, in order). Two are configuration-derived and say `representative-`\nliterally: `resource` and `authorization_servers`.\n\nThat second gap is wider than `/health`'s and the guest's header says so:\n`metadata*` **throws** `:oauth-resource/no-authorization-server` when no\nauthorization server is configured. A real `cloud-itonami-app` in this demo\nhost's state would 500, not serve a degraded document. So the served route\nhere demonstrates a shape; it does not stand in for the real endpoint.\n\n### Gap, restated honestly\n\nThis is **2 of `server.clj`'s ~30 routes**, and only their admission\ndecisions. Everything the base ADR named is unchanged: no sessions, no\nauth, no cookies, no streaming, no structural JSON parsing in-guest, and\nauth / mail+drive sync / the LLM tool-calling loop / WebAuthn / OAuth /\nwallet+payments remain entirely JVM-dependent and untouched.\n`cloud-itonami-app` was not modified and its running server was not touched\nby this slice.\n\n### Proposed next slice\n\n`did_web_core.kotoba` (`GET /.well-known/did.json`, ADR-0040) is the third\nsibling of this same family -- already decision-cored, unauthenticated,\n`[:string :string] -> :bool`. Taking it would make three, and would be the\npoint at which it is worth extracting the now-thrice-repeated parity\nharness into one parameterised script rather than copying it a third time.\n\n## Addendum 3 (2026-08-29) -- four more routes, and the harness extracted to one script\n\nAddendum 2 proposed that at the third route it becomes worth extracting the\nnow-thrice-repeated parity harness into one parameterised script rather than\ncopying it a third time. This slice does that, and takes four routes at once\nbecause four is what makes the extraction pay for itself immediately.\n\n### The extraction\n\n`scripts/cloud-itonami-route-parity.cljs` is now the ONLY implementation of\nthe mechanism: compile the core to `:js-kotoba-v1`, run both runtimes,\ncompare case by case, enforce the floors, report, exit.\n`scripts/cloud-itonami-health-parity.cljs` and\n`scripts/cloud-itonami-oauth-resource-parity.cljs` are now ~30-line\ndelegators that pin `--route health` / `--route oauth-resource` and forward\nthe rest of argv. Their entry points survive because addenda 1 and 2 name\nthem, and the harness reports under each route's original label, so their\noutput is unchanged. What does not survive is a second and third copy of the\nmechanism.\n\nWhat is SHARED is the mechanism. What is deliberately NOT shared is the\nCLAIM: each route's battery stays per-route data in the harness's registry,\nand `health` / `oauth-resource` carry their landed case lists VERBATIM.\nFolding six batteries into one generated list would have been the easy\nrefactor and would have quietly changed what the two landed proofs asserted.\nRe-measured through the refactored harness: health 21/21 and oauth-resource\n28/28, unchanged. A refactor that changes what was proven is a regression\neven at exit 0.\n\n### Three floors, now enforced on every route rather than on the newest one\n\nAddendum 2 introduced two floors that addendum 1 lacked. The value of\nextraction is that they stop being \"the newest script has them\" and become\n\"every route has them\" -- including, retroactively, `/health`, whose original\nscript would have reported PASS on an all-false battery. A third floor is\nadded here:\n\n- **The positive case must be admitted by BOTH runtimes**, checked separately\n  from mismatch counting. A battery on which both sides answer `false`\n  everywhere \"agrees\" with itself while proving nothing.\n- **The detector is mutation-verified, not assumed** -- `--mutant-replace` /\n  `--mutant-with` compile a one-substitution copy of the core for the JS side\n  while the JVM side keeps running the shipped, unmutated namespace.\n- **Cross-admission coverage is computed from the registry and PRINTED for\n  every route.** Each entry declares the one `(method, path)` it admits, so\n  every other route's admitted path is automatically a required negative.\n  Routes declaring `:cross-admission :required` FAIL on any gap; `health` and\n  `oauth-resource` declare `:legacy-partial` because they were landed before\n  these cores existed, and the printed coverage line reports that gap rather\n  than hiding it -- \"飛ばした\" and \"合格した\" stay distinguishable in the\n  output. Extending their batteries is a deliberate act, not a side effect of\n  this refactor.\n\nThe harness also refuses rather than passing on a zero-case battery, and\nrefuses to report a mutation result if the `--mutant-replace` text was not\nfound in the source (a mutation that was never applied must not be reported\nas a mutation that was survived).\n\n### Results\n\nMeasured 2026-08-29 by one run of\n`scripts/cloud-itonami-route-parity.cljs --route all`. \"cross-adm.\" is the\nnumber of other routes' admitted paths carried as negatives in that route's\nbattery.\n\n    route                                   cases  mismatches  cross-adm.  verdict\n    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    health                                   21           0         0/5  PASS\n    oauth-resource                           28           0         1/5  PASS\n    did-web                                  34           0         5/5  PASS\n    did-log                                  34           0         5/5  PASS\n    did-witness                              34           0         5/5  PASS\n    domain-binding-nonce                     34           0         5/5  PASS\n\n    cloud-itonami-route-parity: PASS -- 6 route(s), 0 failures\n    exit 0\n\nAll six admit their positive case under BOTH runtimes (the floor is checked\nseparately from mismatch counting, so a silent all-false battery cannot pass).\n`health` and `oauth-resource` are unchanged at their landed 21 and 28 cases;\ntheir 0/5 and 1/5 cross-admission coverage is the legacy gap described above,\nprinted rather than hidden.\n\n\n### Mutation verification\n\nA detector nobody has watched fail is not known to discriminate. The mutant\nwidens `did-web-route?`'s path test from equality to \"equality OR with a\ntrailing slash\", so the JS side wrongly admits ONE negative while the JVM side\nkeeps running cloud-itonami-app's shipped, unmutated namespace. Run, and its\nreal output -- not asserted from reading the code:\n\n    $ node node_modules/nbb/cli.js scripts/cloud-itonami-route-parity.cljs \\\n        --app-dir <cloud-itonami-app> --route did-web \\\n        --mutant-replace '(string=? path \"/.well-known/did.json\")))' \\\n        --mutant-with '(or (string=? path \"/.well-known/did.json\") (string=? path \"/.well-known/did.json/\"))))'\n\n    cloud-itonami-did-web-parity: MUTANT ACTIVE -- js side compiles a copy with \"(string=? path \\\"/.well-known/did.json\\\")))\" -> \"(or (string=? path \\\"/.well-known/did.json\\\") (string=? path \\\"/.well-known/did.json/\\\"))))\"; jvm side runs the shipped, unmutated namespace.\n    ...\n    MISMATCH [\"GET\" \"/.well-known/did.json/\"] js=true jvm=false\n    ...\n    cloud-itonami-did-web-parity: cross-admission coverage 5/5 sibling admitted paths present as negatives\n\n    cloud-itonami-did-web-parity: FAIL -- 1/34 mismatches:\n    [\"GET\" \"/.well-known/did.json/\"] js=true jvm=false\n\n    cloud-itonami-route-parity: FAIL -- 1/1 route(s) failed\n    exit 1\n\nExactly one case flipped; the detector named THAT case and no other, and exited\nnon-zero. Note what the run also shows: the mutant did not disturb the positive\ncase, which still reported `js=true jvm=true`, so the FAIL came from the\nmismatch path and not from the positive floor. The thing that was broken and\nthe thing that was reported are the same thing -- the check CLAUDE.md asks for\nwhen a mutation is used as evidence.\n\nThe harness also refuses (exit 2, neither pass nor fail) if the\n`--mutant-replace` text is absent from the source, so a mutation that was never\napplied cannot be reported as a mutation that was survived.\n\n\n### What these four batteries could test that the first two could not\n\nThe **strict-prefix pair**. `/.well-known/did.json` and\n`/.well-known/did.jsonl` differ by one trailing character, and\n`server.clj`'s own comment on `did-log-route?` already says so: \"`did.json`\nand `did.jsonl` differ by one character, so both this and the predicate above\ncompare for equality rather than testing a prefix.\" A per-route battery would\nnever generate its sibling's path on its own. Because the harness derives\ncross-admission negatives from the registry, each of these is now the other's\nnegative twice over -- once as a registry sibling, and once as a\none-character neighbour mutation (`p` plus `\"l\"`, `p` minus its last\ncharacter). Both answer `false` under both runtimes.\n\nThis is the general point of doing the four together rather than one per\nslice: there are now five `.well-known` routes across three cores, and\n`domain_binding_core.kotoba`'s own header already states the invariant --\ninverting it \"must leave `/.well-known/did.json` answering -- a different\ncore, a different oracle id.\" That invariant is now measured rather than\nasserted, and it is measured automatically for every route added in future.\n\n### A host/core asymmetry on `did-witness.json`, recorded so it is not misread\n\n`did_web_core.kotoba`'s `did-witness-route?` answers `false` to\n`POST /.well-known/did-witness.json`, under both runtimes, and the battery\nrecords that. **The endpoint nevertheless accepts POST.** `server.clj`'s own\nwrapper is:\n\n    (defn- did-witness-route? [method path]\n      (and (contains? #{\"GET\" \"POST\"} method)\n           (= path \"/.well-known/did-witness.json\")\n           (did-web/did-witness-route? (if (= \"POST\" method) \"GET\" method) path)))\n\n-- it admits both methods and normalises POST to \"GET\" before consulting the\ncore. GET reads the witness proofs; POST is how a witness that signs\nelsewhere files one (ADR-0068).\n\nThis is correct layering, not a parity defect: the core decides \"is this\nrequest that document\", and the host decides method policy. It is recorded\nhere because the case row `[\"POST\" \"/.well-known/did-witness.json\"] =\nfalse` is easy to misread as \"the endpoint rejects POST\", which would be\nwrong. It is also the one place in these six routes where the admission\ndecision alone does not tell you what the endpoint does.\n\n### Why these four are proved for ADMISSION ONLY, and are not served on http-service.mjs\n\nSlices 1 and 2 both served their route on `runtime/http-service.mjs` beside\nthe parity proof, with configuration-derived fields marked\n`representative-`. This slice deliberately does NOT, and the reason is read\nout of `server.clj` rather than assumed:\n\n- **`/.well-known/did.json`** is resolved per-request from the `Host` header\n  through `identity/did-web-domain-for-host`, returns 404 when this\n  deployment publishes no did:web domain, and carries the tenant's real\n  verification keys plus `alsoKnownAs` pointing at its `did:webvh`.\n- **`/.well-known/did.jsonl`** is the did:webvh log -- an append-only,\n  signed history whose entries are the identity.\n- **`/.well-known/did-witness.json`** is witness proofs: signatures whose\n  count a resolver compares against a threshold.\n- **`/.well-known/itonami-domain-binding.json`** serves a per-binding nonce\n  whose whole evidentiary value is that it is secret and unguessable, and\n  which `domain_verification.clj` selects by matching the Host.\n\nFor `/health` a `representative-` field is a placeholder in an otherwise\nhonest document. For these four, the document IS the configuration and the\nkeys: a served demo would be a fabricated DID document, a fabricated signed\nlog, fabricated signatures, and a fabricated nonce. A DID document that looks\nreal and is not is worse than no document, because its failure mode is\nsomebody trusting it. So the claim made here is exactly the claim that can be\nmade honestly -- the ADMISSION DECISION crosses runtimes identically -- and\nthe serving of these bodies is left to a slice that can serve the real ones,\nif there ever is one.\n\nNote also that the parity proof is the part that transfers. The admission\ndecision is the piece `http-service.mjs` needs from these cores; the bodies\nwould come from `credential.clj` / `identity.clj` /\n`domain_verification.clj`, none of which is JVM-free or close to it.\n\n### Gap, restated honestly\n\nThis is now **6 of `server.clj`'s ~30 routes, and only their ADMISSION\nDECISIONS** -- four of the six are not served JVM-free at all, by the choice\nargued above. Everything the base ADR named is still unchanged: no sessions,\nno auth, no cookies, no streaming, no structural JSON parsing in-guest. Mail\nand drive sync, the LLM tool-calling loop, WebAuthn, OAuth and\nwallet/payments remain entirely JVM-dependent and untouched. The bodies of\nall four routes in this slice, and the Host-to-tenant resolution every one of\nthem depends on, remain JVM-only.\n\n`cloud-itonami-app` was not modified by this slice -- it is read-only input,\nits cores are read byte-for-byte and its JVM answers come from running its\nown shipped namespaces -- and its running server on 127.0.0.1:1338 was not\ntouched.\n\nMeasurement conditions: this workstation was at load average ~250-310\nthroughout (many parallel agents), which is why each compile/oracle leg took\nminutes. The load affects wall-clock only; the answers compared are exact\nbooleans.\n\n### Proposed next slice\n\nThe registry now makes adding a route a data edit plus one run, so the next\nslice should stop adding `[:string :string] -> :bool` siblings, which are\nbecoming cheap enough to be uninformative, and take the first route whose\nadmission decision is NOT a two-string equality -- one that reads a header,\na cookie or a session. That is where `:js-kotoba-v1` parity stops being a\nrestatement of string equality and starts testing something the base ADR\nactually named as missing.\n\n## Addendum 4 (2026-08-29) -- correction: the decision core was the wrong unit, and this ADR was steering the next reader toward more of it\n\nOwner challenge, 2026-08-29, on reading addendum 3:\n「kotoba は判定のみ, みたいな refactor はもう古いのでは?」\n\n**Correct, and this workspace had already decided so three days before these\nslices ran.** ADR-2608261100 (accepted 2026-08-26,\n`kotoba-app-vertical-slice-is-the-migration-unit`) demotes decision-core\nextraction from \"the migration unit\" to a **fallback**, and is explicit about\nwhen the fallback is licensed:\n\n> 決定核の抽出が正当なのは、**名指しした backend** がその値を admit できない\n> ときだけである。\n\nand, naming this very backend:\n\n> wasm / web / `:js-kotoba-v1` に対して、最初から判断核へ潰さない。\n> そこでは application を application として書く。\n\nand, in its \"捨てる\" list: 『正しい移行 = 1 判断表』、『Kotoba は判断だけ\n書く言語』.\n\n`:js-kotoba-v1` is the target every slice in this ADR used. It is also\nprecisely the backend for which the fallback is *not* licensed: it admits\nstrings, maps, records, `:document` and `cond` -- `amu`'s own\n`examples/todo-app.kotoba` is a whole interactive application on it, with an\n`init` / `view` / `step` contract. The word-typed admission ceiling that\njustifies a decision core is `native`'s, not this one. So the licensing\ncondition for the shape these slices used was never met here.\n\n### What is withdrawn, and what is not\n\n**Not withdrawn -- the measured property stands.** Six pre-existing decision\ncores give identical admission answers under the JVM/KIR-interpreter oracle\nand under `:js-kotoba-v1` (21/28/34/34/34/34 cases, 0 mismatches,\nmutation-verified). That is a real cross-runtime result and the harness that\nproduces it is real, reusable, and stays.\n\n**Withdrawn -- the framing.** Treating \"another decision core proven\" as the\nmeasure of progress toward running `cloud-itonami-app` without a JVM. Six\nbooleans agreeing across runtimes is evidence about a runtime swap; it is not\nan application moving. This ADR should not have adopted the decision core as\nits unit of progress, and addendum 3 compounded it by planning a seventh.\n\nIn fairness to the record: these six cores were **not created here**. They\nalready existed in `cloud-itonami-app` under its own ADR-0038/0039/0040, made\nwhen the JVM owned the socket. Adopting them was reasonable as a first probe.\nPlanning slice 7 around them was not.\n\n### Addendum 3's \"Proposed next slice\" is superseded\n\nAddendum 3 proposed taking \"the first route whose admission decision is NOT a\ntwo-string equality -- one that reads a header, a cookie or a session.\" That\nis a better boolean, but it is still a boolean: still one\n`... -> :bool` admission predicate, still the unit ADR-2608261100 demoted.\nDo not take it.\n\n### The actual next slice\n\nA `kotoba/app` vertical slice on `:js-kotoba-v1`, written as an application\nrather than collapsed to a predicate: `state + event -> next-state`, with\n`todo-app.kotoba`'s `init` / `view` / `step` as the shape, the guest holding\nproduct semantics (values: strings, maps, records, `:document`, `cond`), and\nthe host holding only mechanism (socket, instantiate, credential, DOM).\nADR-2607279200 decision 5's four-way classification is the default, not\ncore-extraction.\n\nThe concrete blocker is already named by `runtime/http-service.mjs`'s own\nheader and is not a language limit: this host passes a request body across as\na validated-syntactically-JSON `:string`, with no structural parsing inside\nthe guest, because `kotoba-lang/json` has no `.kotoba` port. An application\nthat cannot read its own request body has nothing to be an application\n*about*. That gap -- not a seventh route -- is what the next slice must move,\nand it is under assessment in `kotoba-lang/json` as of this addendum.\n\nIf a future slice does fall back to a decision core, ADR-2608261100 requires\nit to say in the module header what is temporary and which named backend's\nwhich missing admission removes it. None of the six here carry that note,\nbecause none of them needed the fallback.\n"}]
